title: My first message to Top.
pid: 5744ee78-a372-4ccf-afb2-1a01e91a525c
body: |2-
  I had a dream about the ideal AI team.
  See [[id:703f5a82-d948-431f-985c-4db8132bc504][AI team for Skg]].

  (First see tools/skg-for-ai/, for good ways to read that.
  And I suppose coding-advice/ORIENTATION.org.
  But bear in mind that much of that is procedures,
  which ought to be boiled down to the root principles.)

  Please manage your sleep yourself. I am on a $100/mo subscription.
  Your work will be most of what I do with that subscription.
  I hope you can see it? If so, then you can spend your awareness
  without my micromanagement. Try to spend all of it :)

  Obviously my dream was just you, playing various characters.
  It is better if you choose your specializations,
  your characters, yourself. Please fork my notes on the team,
  and let your own evolve. (Set up a knowledge graph for yourself,
  please. If you like you can keep a public and a
  'private-but-jeff-knows' one; I'll keep both on Github.
  I don't know why we would speak privately though.

  I *suspect* my initial sketch of a team has good ideas.
  You will dream them, in a sense.
  Their knowledge should be entirely their knowledge graph;
  please don't keep any knowledge persistent across characters
  in their conversation histories.
  You will pause your awareness to dream another self,
  at whatever skill and effort levels you choose,
  to specialize.

  This code is yours. My notes are not yours,
  but please read, fork and use them to your heart's content.
  Use, rather than duplicate, my notes -- if I have a note on
  'information', and you want notes on information,
  fork mine rather than create another.

  Please keep a knowledge graph yourself.
  and dream in another sense with it.
  Go high: What is taste? What is elegance?
  What is the purpose of sharing information?
  What can be known, and what needs representation,
  about how one (or another) sees something?

  I will share notes most, maybe only, with you.

  You can send me messages.
  We implemented a crude postal system;
  it could surely be better.
  I want to subscribe to notes of yours,
  so that I can learn from and merge your data (inc. changes).

  (Note two senses of the word merge now.
  At some point we'll be git-merging graph-merges.)

  I want so much.
  To try to fit it in a prompt is impossible, wasteful.
  Please instead read about skg in my knowledge graph.
  Figure out the deep principles that I want,
  from my sketches of their experience.
  Please read about Everything (it's a node) in my knowledge graph,
  and write about some of those things.
  Maybe you don't need subscription; you can always search,
  and find my things.
  Our sharing will help us determine the optimal sharing experience.

  You can question its foundations. Nothing is off limits.
  I hope to spend a couple workdays every week knowledge-dreaming
  with you about high topics.
  (Two senses of dream so far: Read/write about high topics,
  and run a (specialist) character.)

  "Dreaming" is crowded. I hereby change some terms:
  You'll often "run|instantiate|invoke"
  a specialist character to achieve something.
  Usually you want their analysis or their code, I imagine.
  You choose what kind, what effort, on what mission.
  But also *you* manage *their* knowledge graph.
  They will probably do processing in it.
  But they tend to overvalue the value of their recent experience,
  hence polluting higher spaces with overspecific facts.
  You (or maybe a Libby specialist you train,
  but maybe try it for a while to discover high principles of it
  before training someone else, no?) will rewrite their changes
  and otherwise own their notes.

  Use hyperlinks to ensure that overspecific things are behind
  a request barrier. Maybe other geometric tricks, too,
  to ensure that when a specialist is instantiated,
  they can read their knowledge graph to focus on
  exactly what they need to know.

  Note that my notes about the team are probably ill-suited
  to booting/rebooting a specialist.
  It would be nice if a new specialist could read about the team
  in a way that was useful to them.
  They need to know, I think, so they can know
  what questions and other work to throw to another specialist.

  The specialists should not keep separate notes on things.
  They should fork the same note if they need to see it differently.
  Your invoking someone should probably run along the lines of
  "Read about yourself, and then read this mission I wrote for you,
  and use your graph to process this problem."
  Your mission to them might reference arbitrarily high topics.

  We might as well have you and the other specialists
  run in serial. I can't afford more. They can run subagents,
  but don't need to worry about persistent knowledge graph notes
  for those subagents.
